            <ArticleTitle>Atypical presentation of Henoch-Schönlein purpura.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>513-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/PEC.0b013e3181b0a46f</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP) is the most common form of vasculitis found in the pediatric population. The most common presenting complaint for children with HSP is a purpuric rash on the lower extremities. Many other organ systems beyond the skin can be involved for children with HSP. We report a case of a 7-year-old girl with HSP who presented with status epilepticus and onset of rash 2 weeks after her initial symptoms.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
